# How much of the system each filing's stack holds, and what decided it. One row per filing: the
# typed extent, and the basis prose required beside it.
#
# Why it exists
#   Without it the emitter would have to assert the answer, and a sentence such as "a division of
#   layers claimed complete", written into every document, says what almost no filing claims: the
#   artifact would say something the filings decline to say, the `invents` state, in the emitter's
#   own prose. No law here reaches a string literal in a `writeln!`, because it is not a count of
#   anything.
#
# Three states, and BPMN renders one
#   `complete` asserts a boundary, `scoped` says somebody established what lies outside and left it
#   out, and `unbounded` says nobody looked. `tLaneSet` has no extent attribute of any kind, and the
#   schema's own gloss already says what is wrong with that: a model that renders them identically
#   cannot tell a bounded selection from an unexamined one. Run reports/scope_declared.sqlc for the
#   split across the corpus.
#
# The basis travels; the observation does not
#   `diagrams/dependences.sqlc` refuses a coupling's observation because some observations carry a
#   numeral onto a page with no unit. No basis carries one, so the same refusal does not apply
#   here, and applying it anyway would be a rule remembered rather than run.
#
# BPMN is finer on the basis
#   BPMN is the finer of the two on the basis and has nothing on the extent. `tLane` carries
#   `partitionElement` and `partitionElementRef` for what a lane set divides by, per lane, where
#   this model files one basis per stack. That is the grain running the other way for once.
-- pm:Stack/pm:StatedScope; carried as the laneSet's own documentation beside the coupling search.
SELECT s.filing, s.extent, s.basis
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/scopes.sqlc)
) s
